I'm trying to sort an array in C++ but I keep using qsort like in C. I heard lambdas are better, but I don't get why. Can't I just stick with function pointers?


TA: 
You're absolutely right that qsort works — it's familiar and gets the job done. But in C++, we prefer tools that catch errors at compile time. For example, qsort uses void pointers, so if you accidentally pass the wrong type, the compiler won’t warn you — it’ll fail at runtime. std::sort, on the other hand, uses templates and knows the type of your container. Also, with lambdas, you can define a comparison inline without writing a separate function. Try this: can you rewrite the sorting call using std::sort and a lambda that returns true when the first element is less than the second? [1](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#Rf-lambda)

